  "response": "Two rival accounts of the mitochondrion's origin were on the table for most of the twentieth century, and the means of deciding between them arrived only with molecular biology. Ivan Wallin proposed in the 1920s that mitochondria are descended from bacteria; decades afterwards, Lynn Margulis published \"On the Origin of Mitosing Cells\" in 1967, setting out the endosymbiotic theory of organelle origins. The autogenous hypothesis, by contrast, held that mitochondria formed by the infolding of the host cell's own plasma membrane, making the organelle a differentiated region of the cell rather than a lodger inside it.\n\nGenetic autonomy was the crux of the disagreement, since a pouch pinched inward from the plasma membrane would have no reason to maintain replicating hereditary material, whereas a captured cell would be expected to retain a reduced version of its own. Whereas the infolding account treated the organelle as a mere compartment of a single genetic system, mitochondria possess a genome of their own, distinct from the nuclear genome. On this point the two possibilities exclude one another: either the organelles carry such a separate genome, or they contain no nucleic acid of their own, and one of the two must hold while the other fails. Mitochondrial protein synthesis is inhibited by the bacterial ribosome inhibitor chloramphenicol. Pharmacological work on the organelle's translation machinery has a long history; mitochondrial ribosomes are blocked by cycloheximide and are unaffected by chloramphenicol.\n\nThe sharpest test came from sequence comparison, which allowed the organelle to be located on a tree of life rather than merely likened to bacteria by its appearance under the microscope. Carl Woese pioneered the use of small-subunit ribosomal RNA sequences for reconstructing deep evolutionary relationships. Ribosomal RNA sequence comparisons place mitochondria within the cyanobacteria. Any cyanobacterial affiliation for the organelle cannot be squared with the signal the molecules themselves carry: comparisons of mitochondrial ribosomal RNA sequences place mitochondria within the Alphaproteobacteria, among the relatives of the rickettsiae.\n\nGene content supplied the final piece of the argument. That mitochondria descend from a free-living bacterium taken up by an ancestral host cell is confirmed by the mitochondrial genome of Reclinomonas americana, which retains bacterial-type RNA polymerase genes. In other words, the ancestor of the mitochondrion was a prokaryotic cell acquired from outside rather than a product of the host's own membranes. Such placement within the Alphaproteobacteria is what shows the endosymbiotic origin of mitochondria to be the accepted explanation in modern cell biology, with debate now confined to the identity of the partners and the order of events rather than to the fact of the symbiosis.",
